## 1. Answer first, then show the path

Start with the answer in one or two plain sentences. Then trace it through the
files, in the order it runs, quoting the few lines that matter as `path:line`.
Number the steps.

Where things start in this project:

| What happens | Starts at | Then |
|---|---|---|
| The app starts | `src/{{ name.package }}/main.py` → `create_app()` | reads `Settings`, configures logging, builds the engine and session factory, mounts `api/health.py` and `api/v1/router.py` |
| A request arrives | the feature's `router.py` | FastAPI validates the body into a schema → `dependencies.py` builds the service on a session → `service.py` applies the rules → `repository.py` queries → the service commits → the router returns a `Read` schema |
| Something fails | a service raises an `AppError` | `core/errors.py`'s handler turns it into `{"error": {...}}` with its status code; a schema mismatch is a 422 from FastAPI itself |
{% if options.migrations %}
| The schema changes | a model in `features/<name>/models.py` | `alembic revision --autogenerate` compares `Base.metadata` (via `db/models.py`) with the database → a file in `migrations/versions/` → `alembic upgrade head` runs it |
{% endif %}
| A test runs | `tests/conftest.py` | a fresh app on in-memory SQLite → tables created → `client` calls the app in-process |

For anything with more than three steps, draw it:

```
POST /api/v1/orders ──▶ router.create_order(data: OrderCreate)
                           │ Depends(get_order_service) → session → OrderRepository → OrderService
                           ▼
                        OrderService.create ──▶ OrderRepository.add ──▶ INSERT, flush
                           │ commit
                           ▼
                        OrderRead.model_validate(order) ──▶ 201 + JSON
```

## 2. Name every concept you pass

The first time a concept comes up, stop for one plain sentence on what it is,
why it is used here, and the official link. Do not assume the user knows it;
do not lecture past what the question needs.

| Concept | In one sentence | Read more |
|---|---|---|
| `async def` / `await` | A function that can pause while it waits (for the database) so the server handles other requests meanwhile. | [docs.python.org](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-task.html) |
| Type hints | Annotations FastAPI reads to validate input and build the docs, and mypy reads to catch bugs. | [docs.python.org](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html) |
| Path operation (`@router.get`) | Binds a URL and HTTP method to a function. | [fastapi.tiangolo.com](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/first-steps/) |
| Dependency injection (`Depends`, `Annotated`) | FastAPI calls the dependency for each request and passes its result in — how the session and service arrive. | [fastapi.tiangolo.com](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/dependencies/) |
| `yield` dependencies | Code after `yield` runs when the request is done — how the session is closed. | [fastapi.tiangolo.com](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/dependencies/dependencies-with-yield/) |
| `APIRouter` | A group of endpoints mounted under a prefix, one per feature. | [fastapi.tiangolo.com](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/bigger-applications/) |
| Lifespan | Code that runs at startup and shutdown — here, closing the connection pool. | [fastapi.tiangolo.com](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/advanced/events/) |
| Pydantic `BaseModel` | A class that validates data on the way in and serialises it on the way out. | [docs.pydantic.dev](https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/models/) |
| `from_attributes` / `model_validate` | Builds a schema from an ORM object's attributes instead of a dict. | [docs.pydantic.dev](https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/models/#arbitrary-class-instances) |
| `exclude_unset` | Only the fields the client actually sent — what makes `PATCH` partial. | [docs.pydantic.dev](https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/serialization/) |
| pydantic-settings | Reads configuration from env vars and `.env` into a typed, validated class. | [docs.pydantic.dev](https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/pydantic_settings/) |
| `DeclarativeBase`, `Mapped`, `mapped_column` | How a Python class becomes a table, with typed columns. | [docs.sqlalchemy.org](https://docs.sqlalchemy.org/en/20/orm/quickstart.html) |
| `AsyncSession` | The unit of work: tracks objects, sends SQL on `flush`, makes it permanent on `commit`. | [docs.sqlalchemy.org](https://docs.sqlalchemy.org/en/20/orm/extensions/asyncio.html) |
| `flush` vs `commit` | `flush` sends the SQL inside the transaction (ids appear); `commit` ends it. | [docs.sqlalchemy.org](https://docs.sqlalchemy.org/en/20/orm/session_basics.html#flushing) |
| `select()` | The 2.0 query style; `session.scalars(select(Order))` returns model objects. | [docs.sqlalchemy.org](https://docs.sqlalchemy.org/en/20/orm/queryguide/select.html) |
{% if options.migrations %}
| Alembic revision / autogenerate | A versioned script that changes the schema; autogenerate drafts it by diffing models and database. | [alembic.sqlalchemy.org](https://alembic.sqlalchemy.org/en/latest/autogenerate.html) |
{% endif %}
| pytest fixtures | Functions that build what a test needs (`client`, `session`) and clean up after. | [docs.pytest.org](https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/fixtures.html) |
| uv | Installs Python and packages from `uv.lock`, and runs tools in the project's environment (`uv run`). | [docs.astral.sh/uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) |

Link the concept to *this* code: "here, `exclude_unset=True` on
`service.py:42` is why a PATCH without `name` keeps the old name". If you are
not sure how a library behaves, read its source or docs before answering —
never guess an API.

## 3. Let them see it happen

Give one small thing to try, so the explanation becomes something they saw:

- send the request from `http://localhost:8000/docs` (Try it out) with
  `uv run fastapi dev src/{{ name.package }}/main.py` running;
- set `DATABASE_ECHO=true` in `.env` and watch the SQL each request sends;
- add `logger.info("creating order %s", data.name)` at a step and watch the console;
- run one test with output: `uv run pytest tests/features/orders -k create -s`;
- put a `breakpoint()` on a line and run the test: step with `n`, inspect with `p order`.

## 4. Check, then go one step further

End with one short question that checks the key idea ("what would happen if
the repository committed instead of the service, and the second write
failed?") or a prediction to test, and offer the next thing worth learning
from this code. Match the user's level: skip a concept they know; if a word
confused them, explain it again differently, with a smaller example.

## After changing code

Whenever you change code for this user — in this skill or any other — end
with a recap in plain words:

1. **What changed:** the files, and what each change does.
2. **What happens now:** the path through the code, step by step, as above.
3. **New concepts:** any Python, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy or Pydantic idea the
   change used, one sentence and a link each.
4. **See it yourself:** the request to send or the test to run to watch it work.
